## Gates verified on current tree

- packages/cli/src/close.test.ts: PASS (7/7 behaviours, exit 0)
- packages/cli/src/rules/process.test.ts: PASS (17/17 behaviours incl. 6
  checkpointStale behaviours, exit 0)

Both suites pass, but coverage does not reach the actually-reachable CLI
entry points (`runClose`, `rebuildDossier`) — every close.test.ts assertion
exercises only the pure `closeChecks`/`executeClose` functions, never
`runClose` (the function `main.ts` wires to `bisellium close`). That gap
hid two functional bugs, both reproduced live against the built CLI:

## Bug 1 — dossier-rebuild failure is swallowed; close reports success anyway

`runClose` (packages/cli/src/close.ts:75) calls `rebuildDossier(repo)` and
discards its boolean return value unconditionally:

    rebuildDossier(repo);
    console.log(`${opusId} closed — dossier rebuilt`);
    return { exitCode: 0 };

Reproduced: pointed --repo at a fixture with a `docs/design/dossier/build.sh`
that exits 1, ran `bisellium close`. Opus transitioned to `done`, command
printed "W-500 closed — dossier rebuilt" and exited 0, despite the rebuild
script failing. This is the exact failure mode the opus's own
`process.checkpoint` rule (same opus, packages/cli/src/rules/process.ts)
was written to catch — code/state lands, checkpoint artifact silently
doesn't — except now produced by the automation meant to prevent it, and
reported as a success. The commit message for 19f2353 ("executeClose ...
rebuilds dossier HTML") also does not match the code: `rebuildDossier` is
called from `runClose`, never from `executeClose`, so any caller reusing
`executeClose` directly (e.g. a future autonomous patron sella, this
opus's own stated later deliverable) gets no dossier rebuild at all.

## Bug 2 — closeChecks's CLOSEABLE set doesn't match lifecycle's real states

`closeChecks` (close.ts:12) allows `{"building", "reviewing", "greenlit"}`.
The lifecycle state ids (docs/ADOPTION.md:15, and `runDone`'s own
`DONE_FROM_STATES` in packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:150) are
`{"building", "verifying", "review"}` — there is no "reviewing" state, and
"greenlit" is a pre-build state `runDone` never accepts. Net effect,
reproduced live: `bisellium close` refuses an opus in `state: verifying` or
`state: review` — the two states a "close after checks pass" command exists
to serve — while accepting a nonexistent "reviewing" (dead code) and a
"greenlit" opus that will fail one step later inside `runDone` anyway. Only
`state: building` actually works end to end. No test in close.test.ts
exercises "verifying" or "review" as an input state; behaviour 0 only
covers "building".

## Brief conformance

process.checkpoint rule (in-scope deliverable): matches the brief exactly —
6 behaviours (advise-level, opera-changed-without-handoff), wired into
checkProcess via git diff HEAD~1 HEAD. No issues found here.

`bisellium close` / executeClose (opus's own stated stretch work, not the
brief's in-scope item, but shipped and offered for review as such): fails
its own purpose on two of its three legitimate entry states and lies about
dossier-rebuild success on the one state that does work.

## Verdict

FAIL. Both bugs are reproducible against the shipped code, not mutations,
and both sit directly on the opus's stated intent (guarantee checkpoint
artifacts land; close an opus once its gates pass). Fix: align CLOSEABLE
with DONE_FROM_STATES (or import it), surface/propagate rebuildDossier's
result as a non-zero exit + error message, and add runClose-level tests
(success, dossier-failure, verifying/review states) so the CLI entry point
itself is under test, not just the pure helpers.
